=== tests/rollback-1.5.0.tests.sh (parent commit 8802505, pre-T-001, isolated `git worktree add
--detach <tmpdir> 8802505` checkout) ===
$ bash tests/rollback-1.5.0.tests.sh ; echo "EXIT=$?"
--- stdout ---
Not currently on any branch.
nothing to commit, working tree clean
--- stderr ---
(empty)
EXIT=1

Both sides fail identically: exit 1, and the visible text IS the failure —
it is `git commit`'s own "nothing to commit, working tree clean" report
("On branch ..." vs. "Not currently on any branch." differs only because
the parent-commit side is a detached-HEAD checkout; git's own cosmetic
current-branch line, not a substantive difference). No "not ok:"/`fail()`
line is ever reached on either side, because this suite's own
`set -euo pipefail` aborts the WHOLE script at the exact internal
`git -C "$canonical_release" ... commit -qm t008-release` call inside its
TEST-006 block (traced directly with `bash -x tests/rollback-1.5.0.tests.sh`
in the worktree: the failing line is the `commit` immediately after that
block's `git -C "$canonical_release" add .`) — the abort IS a real,
nonzero-exit-code failure, just not one of the suite's own named
assertions. Root cause (confirmed by direct reproduction of the same
clone + re-copy + add + commit sequence in isolation): TEST-006 clones
`$ROOT` into a fresh temp dir, then re-copies the same 7 fixed paths
(`contracts/rollback-1.5.0.json`, `scripts/rollback-1.5.0.sh`,
`scripts/rollback-1.5.0.ps1`, `tests/rollback-1.5.0.tests.sh`,
`tests/rollback-1.5.0.tests.ps1`, `tests/run-all.sh`, `tests/run-all.ps1`)
from `$ROOT` on top of that fresh clone before `git add . && git commit`,
expecting the recopy to produce a new commit. On any perfectly clean
checkout (worktree or not — bytes copied are identical to what the clone
already has, since both come from the same `$ROOT` HEAD), `git add .`
stages nothing, so the internal `git commit` has nothing to commit and
exits 1 with exactly this message. This working-tree-state assumption
does not hold for any clean checkout, independent of branch content —
consistent with both sides failing identically here.

